Cruise WS23203 (2023-07-22)
This report maps the shallowest depth at which the interpolated dissolved oxygen field falls below a chosen threshold (hypoxic boundary). Use the slider on the map to change the O₂ threshold; grid cells are colored by depth in meters.


Observation and field summary
data.frame(
  casts = length(unique(obs$cast_id)),
  observation_rows = nrow(obs),
  field_grid_points = if (is.null(field)) NA_integer_ else nrow(field),
  oxygen_range_mg_l = if ("dissolved_oxygen" %in% names(obs)) {
    range(obs$dissolved_oxygen, na.rm = TRUE)
  } else {
    c(NA_real_, NA_real_)
  }
)


  casts observation_rows field_grid_points oxygen_range_mg_l
1    84             7027            158040            1.2908
2    84             7027            158040            5.9850

Hypoxic depth map
Pan and zoom to explore where oxygen drops below the threshold. Each colored cell shows the shallowest depth (closest to the surface) where interpolated O₂ is below the threshold — not the depth where O₂ equals the threshold. When the threshold exceeds surface oxygen across the domain, expect uniform shallow depths (~2 m, the top of the interpolated grid). Hover a cell for its depth in meters. Use the depth color scale inputs to adjust the color bar limits.


Interactive map of shallowest hypoxic depth
if (is.null(field)) {
  cat(
    "Interpolated field not found. Run `make interpolate CRUISE=",
    params$batch_value,
    "` first.",
    sep = ""
  )
} else {
  map &lt;- plot_hypoxic_depth_map(field, obs)
  if (is.null(map)) {
    cat("No interpolated field grid available for this cruise.")
  } else {
    map
  }
}

